Why Is Predistortion Becoming Important in RF Power Amplifier Design?

RF power amplifiers are essential components in wireless communication transmitters because they increase signal power before transmission through an antenna.

However, operating an amplifier near its maximum output level can introduce nonlinear behavior, including gain compression and phase distortion. These effects can reduce signal quality and create unwanted emissions outside the intended frequency channel.

Predistortion addresses this challenge by modifying the input signal before amplification. The correction compensates for the nonlinear characteristics of the amplifier, helping the output signal remain closer to the required linear response.

Piecewise linear techniques use multiple segments to approximate the inverse transfer function. This approach allows correction behavior to be adjusted across different input-power levels.

The technology is relevant to communication systems where spectral performance, energy efficiency and output power must be balanced.

